Well, if I'm not mistaken, having an INT of 25 should be enough for
you to read any uncursed spellbook of identify.  As it is now, you
would spend power points like mad, and probably starve to death if
you identified everything you had.  Maybe a twist I could suggest
would be one of the following:

-- Wizards, being specially trained in the Arts, intrinsically
spend fewer power points when casting spells.   OR

-- When you cast a spell, the power points you spend depend on
your INT and Level (regardless of class).  Something like what
we have now would be the expenditure for 3-12 INT.  Better INTs
would start to make spells cheaper.
   I originally considered throwing WIS into here also, but WIS
already directly influences how many POW you have in the first
place.       OR

-- Each time you reinforce a spell (Your knowledge of that spell
is keener), the cost for that particular spell goes down.
I don't know off hand how Nethack handles known spells, but
one could replace a 'know-spell' boolean with a 'spell-cost' int.

Each of these has the effect that those who spend a lot of time
practicing spellcraft become better and more efficient at it...
kind of like 'exercising' your spells.

